commit e68ea604c	a ceiling on the rows of a segment of a key_cut build, not a speed: test/sql/col_key_cut_segment_cap.sql builds 3.2 million rows at the real 128-segment directory under an array limit and holds the bound; no statement of the registry reads the build arrays
commit 10ce9e0e6	a plan choice between the walk of the order array and the units for a list page under a second test (2.68 to 1.81 and 3.63 to 1.96 million instructions on 25,000 rows to a group); the registry has no list page under a second filter on a key_order group, test/sql/col_topn_walk_price.sql pins the choice on 1,000,000 rows
commit 783b4d09f	a ceiling on the memory of a key_cut build, not a speed: test/sql/col_key_cut_segment_cap.sql builds 3.2 million rows, a third of them one value, under an array limit at the real 128-segment directory, and test/sql/col_key_cut_hot_value.sql holds the rule; no statement of the registry reads the build arrays
commit ae68778a0	which step of a rewrite chooses the options of a sift_col index again from the data, not a speed: test/sql/col_key_cut_redecide.sql (worker off) and col_key_cut_redecide_async.sql (worker on) hold the choice; no statement of the registry rewrites a churned index
group the second order of the order arrays (`order_array_group = 'auto'`) also by an integer column that a non-unique b-tree	a choice of the build held by test/sql/col_gorder_entity_group.sql (failing first: no second order for any b-tree shape); measured on scratch tables of 150,000 to 5,000,000 rows, docs/INDEX-RECIPES.md; no registry case builds a table with a b-tree on a measure and a timestamp
group `sift.scope_create` builds a copy of a fact table keyed by a scope key	the copy is measured by the benchmark of docs/INDEX-RECIPES.md (join against copy on a generated fixture); a bench case of the registry follows with the fuzz and partition work
group `CREATE INDEX` and `REINDEX` of a `sift_col` index on a table shaped like a scope copy take the first column	a choice of the build held by test/sql/col_scope_build_rule.sql (failing first: the foreign key column was the key_order of the copy); the instructions are measured in the commit on the generated fixture of the scope copy
figure One key of a copy of 277,389 rows and 60 keys in arrival order	measured with perf stat on one backend (user instructions, median of 3, maintenance workers 0 for the builds) and EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) on the scope copy fixture of the study, not from records; the choice is pinned by test/sql/col_scope_build_rule.sql
figure the generic plan of a statement whose partition key is bound by a parameter	measured with perf stat on one backend (user instructions of EXPLAIN (ANALYZE) of EXECUTE, median of 3), plan and execution ms from the same plan, a scope copy of 1,831,808 rows in 60 list partitions, custom, generic and auto plan_cache_mode; no case of the registry prepares a statement on a partitioned copy: test agg_scope_param_plan holds the plans and rows
figure `sift.scope_follow_pairs` keeps a table of unique (member, scope key) pairs equal to a view	the pair table is measured by the benchmark of docs/INDEX-RECIPES.md (expansion, request and edit costs on a generated fixture, warm and cold, against the view); a bench case of the registry follows with the fuzz and partition work
group `sift.scope_create` takes an `options` list for the copy's index	the copy is measured by the benchmark of docs/INDEX-RECIPES.md (join against copy on a generated fixture); a bench case of the registry follows with the fuzz and partition work
figure takes an `options` list for the copy's index	measured with perf stat on one backend (user instructions, median of three runs, warm) on a 200,000-row fact table and its scope copy of one key with 66,000 rows, a jsonb map read by `jsonb_each`: the paths chosen from the documents (a sequential scan), the declared wildcard (SiftAgg) and the same copy before and after VACUUM; test/sql/col_scope_options.sql pins the plan and the answers
figure a statement whose `IN`, `EXISTS` or join to a table of unique pairs	measured with perf stat on one backend (user instructions of the statement, median of 3) on the generated fixture of test/sql/agg_probe_set_pricing.sql scaled to 2,000,000 facts and 20,000 ids; the plan labels per size of the set are held by that spec and test/corpus/plans.tsv
figure the plan that probes a table of unique pairs for each key the scan meets, made of an `IN` over it	measured with perf stat on one backend (user instructions of the statement, median of 3) on the generated fixture of test/sql/agg_probe_range_pricing.sql scaled to 2,000,000 facts and 20,000 ids; the plan labels per range are held by that spec
figure `sift.scope_follow_pairs(.., 'pair', 'tenant_id')` refreshes the (member, scope key) pairs	measured with perf stat on one backend (user instructions of one statement in a rolled back transaction, median of 3) on the generated fixture of docs/INDEX-RECIPES.md 'What an assignment edit costs by pair and by key'; the exactness is held by test/sql/col_scope_pairs.sql
figure Added: pg_sift: EXPERIMENTAL scope index	measured on a generated fixture of the scope harness (100,000 events, 5,000 entities, 40 tenants, instructions:u of one backend, median of 5), which the bench registry does not run: the planner recognition of the join forms is not in this release, so no registry statement reaches a scope read; test/sql/col_scope_read.sql and col_scope_dedupe.sql compare the rows with the join
EXPERIMENTAL scope index: `CREATE INDEX .. USING sift_col	the harness fixture of the scope tests measures it (1 key 0.7 to 1.0 against 2.6 to 3.4 million instructions, +107% index size); a registry case follows the planner recognition of the join forms, which is not in this release
figure a scope copy on the pairs deletes the copy's rows of the pairs that go only for the pairs	measured with perf stat on one backend (user instructions of one statement in a rolled back transaction, median of 3) on the generated fixture of docs/INDEX-RECIPES.md 'What the pairs that go cost with a copy'; the plans are held by test/sql/col_scope_pair_delete_plan.sql
figure the statement that keeps a scope copy exact when pairs are added reads the facts	measured with perf stat on one backend (user instructions of one statement in a rolled back transaction, median of 1 to 3) on the generated fixture of docs/INDEX-RECIPES.md 'What the pairs that come in cost with a copy'; the probes are counted, not timed, by test/sql/col_scope_pair_insert_plan.sql
a statement that joins the scanned table to the scope relation of a scope index	not a speed claim by itself: it picks the scope read, whose cost is measured with the scope index (docs/COL-SCOPE.md); the plan choice and the rows are held by test/sql/agg_scope_recognition.sql and the scope family of the differential fuzz
figure a read of a scope key whose members have pairs that came in since the scope segments were written	measured with perf stat on one backend (user instructions of the statement, median of 3, warm) and EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) on the scope index fixture of 1,000,000 facts, 20,000 members and 500 keys with a read of a key of 995 members after 1, 10, 30, 100 and 1,000 members had pairs inserted, the patch always against the priced read; the choice is pinned by test/sql/col_scope_patch_price.sql (the route, the share of the table a patch reads and the answer against the join)
figure Improved: pg_sift: `ORDER BY ts LIMIT n` (with an `OFFSET` inside the ends, in either direction)	measured on the 12-column lab index of 500,000 rows cut by a tenant column (instructions:u of one backend, blocks hit and read, median of runs, build and the clean A/B of the commit before and after on clean installs), which the bench registry does not run: no case builds an index cut by another column over a timestamp in heap order; test/sql/col_gorder_heap_order_ends.sql holds the plan (the global order) and the rows, and the differential fuzz family gorder covers the table
figure a scan of every key of a scope index reads the scope units key by key	measured with perf stat on one backend (user instructions of the statement, median of 5, warm) and EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) on the scope index fixture of 300,000 events, 5,000 members and 50 tenants, the sum and count of every tenant by the derived key with 0 and 2,000 buffered rows and with 20 changed pairs, against the join; the answer against the join is pinned by test/sql/col_scope_groupkey.sql
figure a read of a scope key through an index whose write buffer holds rows	measured with perf stat on one backend (user instructions of the statement, median of 5, warm) and EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) on the scope index fixture of 300,000 events, 5,000 members and 50 tenants, a read of one tenant with 0, 2,000 and 20,000 buffered rows and with 20 changed pairs, the delta against the join and against the clean read; the route and the answer against the join are pinned by test/sql/col_scope_delta.sql
figure the pairs of every key a scope read lists come from the leaves of the journal index	measured with perf stat on one backend (user instructions of the statement, median of 5, warm) on the scope index fixture of 300,000 events, 5,000 members and 50 tenants, the listing of the 7,000 pairs through the btree of the relation and through the leaves of its journal index; the answer against the join is pinned by test/sql/col_scope_groupkey.sql and test/sql/col_scope_twohop_patch.sql
figure the copies of the write buffers' rows a scope read expands	measured with perf stat on one backend (user instructions of the statement, median of 5, warm) on the scope index fixture of 300,000 events, 5,000 members and 50 tenants, the sum and count of every tenant by the derived key with 2,000 buffered rows before and after the sorts, against the join; the answer against the join is pinned by test/sql/col_scope_groupkey.sql and test/sql/col_scope_delta.sql
figure the rows of a key whose pairs changed since the scope segments were written	measured with perf stat on one backend (user instructions of the statement, median of 5, warm) and EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) on the scope index fixture of 300,000 events, 5,000 members and 50 tenants, the sum and count of every tenant by the derived key and of one tenant with 20 and 200 changed pairs, against the join, and the one tenant read with the patch forced (sift.test_scope_patch); the answer against the join is pinned by test/sql/col_scope_changed_pairs.sql and test/sql/col_scope_groupkey.sql
figure the scope key of a statement that the scope index reads may be the rows of a table	measured with perf stat on one backend (user instructions, median of 3) and EXPLAIN (ANALYZE) on the generated scope fixture of the planner recognition tests (300,000 events, 5,000 entities, 50 tenants, one user with 1, 3 and 10 keys), join as written against the scope read; the bench registry does not run a table of keys yet
figure the planner reads `JOIN scope_rel s ON s.member = f.fk GROUP BY s.key` (every tenant at once	measured with perf stat on one backend (user instructions of EXPLAIN (ANALYZE) of EXECUTE, median of 3, force_generic_plan) on the generated 300,000-event fixture of the scope study, clean and after 20 and 180 pairs were deleted and inserted again, the arms join (sift.agg_scope off), scope (sift.test_scope_patch on) and the node's own choice, with the md5 of the rows equal; the choice between the two is pinned by test agg_scope_group_key and agg_scope_choice, no case of the registry has a scope index
figure On a lab table of 950,000 rows churned by 2,000 inserts, updates and soft deletes a batch	measured with perf stat on one backend (user instructions of EXPLAIN (ANALYZE), second run in a fresh backend, p50 of 26 batch ends, a fresh build of a copy of the table as the reference) over one rewrite cycle of the churn lab, the same window with and without the fold, maintenance workers on; the cases of the registry read a settled index and do not churn it: the exactness of the fold is pinned by test/sql/col_fold_marks.sql, test/isolation/specs/col_fold_marks.spec and test/t/col_fold_marks_standby.sh
